course: biology
lesson: 7
title: Antarctica: penguins, seals and the coldest place on Earth
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Antarctica is a continent at the bottom of the world, buried under ice more than 4 km thick in places, so high, dry and dark in winter that it holds the record for the coldest temperature ever measured.
explain: Almost all its life is in and around the sea: the Southern Ocean swarms with krill, tiny shrimp-like animals that feed whales, seals, penguins, fish and seabirds, so krill are the bottom of almost every Antarctic menu.
explain: The animals beat the cold by teamwork and clever bodies: emperor penguins huddle in a blizzard and take turns in the warm middle, icefish carry natural antifreeze, and seals keep breathing holes open in the ice.
explain: Christmas cards love polar bears and penguins playing together, but they never meet: polar bears live in the Arctic in the north, penguins almost only in the south, and the warm tropical oceans lie between them.
explain: In Happy Feet the emperor penguins find their partners by singing, and that part is truer than it looks: real emperor penguins recognise their mate and their chick by voice in a crowd of thousands.
fact: Male emperor penguins huddle through the Antarctic winter in winds up to about 200 km per hour and temperatures below minus 40 degrees, each balancing one egg on his feet under a warm fold of belly skin for about two months without eating.
fact: Emperor penguins find their partners and chicks among thousands of others by recognising their calls.
fact: Penguins toboggan on their bellies because sliding is faster and uses less energy than waddling, and gentoo penguins are the fastest swimming penguins, at about 35 km per hour underwater.
fact: Polar bears live only in the Arctic and penguins almost only in the southern half of the world, so they never meet in the wild; the Galapagos penguin lives right at the equator, kept cool by cold currents.
fact: The leopard seal, about 3.5 metres long, eats krill, fish, penguins and other seals, and its back teeth lock together like a sieve to strain krill.
fact: Antarctic krill are shrimp-like animals about 6 cm long, and all of them together may weigh hundreds of millions of tonnes, possibly more than all the humans on Earth.
fact: The blue whale, up to about 30 metres long, is the largest animal that has ever lived; it gulps krill and seawater and pushes the water out through comb-like baleen, eating around 4 tonnes of krill a day; its heart, the biggest of any animal, is reported to weigh about 180 kg.
fact: The coldest temperature ever measured at a weather station was about minus 89 degrees Celsius, at Vostok Station in Antarctica in 1983.
fact: The Antarctic ice sheet is over 4 km thick in places and holds about 70 per cent of the world's fresh water.
fact: Weddell seals live farther south than any other mammal, can hold their breath for over an hour and keep breathing holes open by scraping the ice with their teeth.
fact: Antarctic icefish are the only animals with backbones that have no red blood cells, so their blood is almost clear; very cold water holds plenty of oxygen, and many Antarctic fish have antifreeze in their blood.
fact: The wandering albatross has the longest wingspan of any living bird, up to about 3.5 metres, and glides for hours on the winds of the Southern Ocean.
fact: Scott's and Shackleton's huts still stand after over 100 years because cold and dryness stop rotting, and no people live in Antarctica permanently, only scientists and support staff.
fact: Adelie penguins build nests of stones that lift the eggs above meltwater, and because good stones are hard to find they steal them from their neighbours; penguins can drink seawater because a gland above the eye removes extra salt.
exercise: Why do emperor penguins huddle together in a blizzard? | Huddling cuts the heat each penguin loses | They are hiding together from the hungry polar bears | They are waiting to be fed by the females | They are keeping the snow off the ice
exercise: How do emperor penguin dads keep their egg warm? | On their feet, under a fold of skin | In a nest of stones | Buried deep in the snow | Tucked under one flipper
exercise: Why do penguins slide on their bellies? | It is faster and uses less energy than waddling | Their legs are too weak to walk at all on the ice | It keeps their bellies cool | It helps them find fish swimming under the ice
exercise: Which penguin is the fastest swimmer? | The gentoo, at about 35 km per hour | The emperor, at about 100 km per hour | The Galapagos penguin, at about 80 km per hour | All penguins swim at exactly the same speed
exercise: Why do polar bears and penguins never meet in the wild? | Polar bears live in the Arctic and penguins almost only in the south | Polar bears are afraid of penguins and always run away from them | Penguins hide whenever polar bears come | They meet only in winter, in the dark
exercise: Which penguin lives right at the equator? | The Galapagos penguin | The emperor penguin | The Adelie penguin | No penguin lives near the equator
exercise: How does a leopard seal catch krill? | Its back teeth lock together like a sieve | It sucks them up through a long tube | It stuns them with a loud bark | It catches them one by one with its flippers, very fast
exercise: Why are krill so important in the Southern Ocean? | Whales, seals, penguins, fish and seabirds all eat them | They clean the ice so it stays white | They are the only animals in the sea that make the oxygen we breathe | They keep the water warm with their swarms
exercise: How does a blue whale keep the krill but get rid of the water? | It pushes the water out through comb-like baleen | It swallows everything and spits the krill back out | It filters the water through its gills like a fish | It sucks the krill through its blowhole
exercise: What is the largest animal that has ever lived? | The blue whale | The biggest dinosaur | The whale shark | The giant squid
exercise: What is the coldest temperature ever measured at a weather station? | About minus 89 degrees Celsius, at Vostok in Antarctica | About minus 40 degrees Celsius, at the North Pole | About minus 200 degrees Celsius, at the South Pole | About minus 60 degrees Celsius, in Siberia
exercise: How do Weddell seals breathe under the frozen sea? | They scrape breathing holes in the ice with their teeth | They breathe the air bubbles trapped under the ice | They have gills as well as lungs | They never come up to breathe at all in the long winter
exercise: Why is an Antarctic icefish's blood almost clear? | It has no red blood cells | It is full of seawater instead of blood | It is frozen into ice | It is white because of the cold water
exercise: Why do many Antarctic fish not freeze solid? | They have natural antifreeze in their blood | They swim too fast to freeze | They sleep in the warm mud on the seabed all winter | Salt water can never get cold enough
exercise: Which bird has the longest wingspan of any living bird? | The wandering albatross | The emperor penguin | The Andean condor | The bald eagle
exercise: Why are explorers' huts in Antarctica still standing after 100 years? | Cold and dryness stop bacteria and fungi rotting them | They were built of thick stone and steel | Scientists rebuild them every single year | They were frozen inside the ice until a few years ago, when it melted
exercise: Why do Adelie penguins steal stones from their neighbours? | Stones lift the eggs above meltwater and are scarce | They eat the stones to help them digest fish | They use them as weapons | They collect shiny stones just for fun, like magpies
exercise: How can penguins drink seawater? | A gland above the eye gets rid of extra salt | They spit the salt out of their beaks | Their stomachs turn the salt into fat for the winter | They only drink rainwater
test: «christmas card's got polar bears and penguins being mates, aww» What is right? | They never meet: polar bears live in the north, penguins in the south | They are friends in the Arctic | Polar bears eat penguins every day | They meet every winter at the equator, halfway between the poles
test: «happy feet's nonsense, penguins don't use their voices» What is right? | Emperor penguins really do find their mates and chicks by their calls | Penguins have no voice at all | Penguins find their mates by smell only, as all the penguins look the same | Penguins never find each other again
test: What would happen to whales, seals and penguins if the Antarctic krill disappeared? | Many would starve, because krill are their main food | Nothing, they eat only big fish | They would eat ice instead | They would all swim north and move to the Arctic, where there is more food
test: «emperor penguin dads are lazy, they just stand there for 2 months» What is the best answer? | They are guarding the egg on their feet in the cold and eating nothing | They stand still because they are frozen solid | They are asleep and the egg looks after itself | They eat snow all the time instead of fish, so they never get hungry at all
test: Count them: five emperor penguin dads, each with one egg on his feet. How many feet and how many eggs? | 10 feet and 5 eggs | 5 feet and 10 eggs | 20 feet and 5 eggs | 10 feet and 10 eggs
test: «blue whale's heart is the size of a football, tops» What is closer to the truth? | It is reported to weigh about 180 kg, the biggest heart of any animal | It is the size of a pea | It is about the size of a human heart | Whales have no heart
test: «don't drink seawater, penguins'd die too» Which is right about penguins? | Penguins can drink seawater, because a gland removes the extra salt | Penguins never drink at all | Penguins only drink melted snow | Penguins die if they swallow even a little seawater, so they keep their beaks shut
test: «why would a fish have see-through blood, that's well weird» Which is the best answer? | Icefish have no red blood cells, and icy water holds lots of oxygen | It is a trick of the light in the ice | It is ill and losing its blood | The blood freezes into clear ice as soon as the fish swims into cold water
test: «antarctica's got towns innit, people live there» What is right? | No one lives there for good; only scientists and support staff stay | Yes, it has big towns and schools | Yes, people farm sheep there | No, nobody has ever been there
test: «why's an albatross never flap, is it lazy» Which is the best answer? | It glides on the strong winds over the waves and saves energy | It cannot flap its wings at all | It is sleeping in the air | It only glides when it is ill or very tired after a long flight
test: «penguin nicked a stone off its mate, cheeky sod» Why do Adelie penguins steal stones? | Good stones lift the eggs above meltwater and are hard to find | They eat stones for breakfast | They throw stones at seals | They like shiny things like magpies and hide them in their nests
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distribution of polar bears and penguins? | Polar bears are confined to the Arctic, whereas penguins inhabit almost exclusively the southern hemisphere | Polar bears and penguins inhabit the same regions of Antarctica | Penguins are confined to the Arctic, whereas polar bears inhabit Antarctica and the islands of the Southern Ocean | Both species are found principally at the equator
exam: Identify the principal function of baleen in the feeding of the blue whale. | Baleen retains the krill whilst permitting the expulsion of seawater from the mouth | Baleen serves to masticate large fish | Baleen serves to pump air into the lungs | Baleen serves to produce sound for echolocation
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the lowest temperature recorded at a meteorological station. | A temperature of approximately minus eighty-nine degrees Celsius was recorded at Vostok Station, Antarctica, in 1983 | A temperature of approximately minus forty degrees Celsius was recorded at the North Pole | A temperature of approximately minus two hundred degrees Celsius was recorded at the South Pole during the polar night | No temperature below minus fifty degrees Celsius has ever been recorded
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the blood of the Antarctic icefish is almost colourless. | The icefish possesses no red blood cells, the cold water furnishing sufficient dissolved oxygen | The blood of the icefish consists of seawater | The blood of the icefish is permanently frozen | The icefish possesses no blood whatsoever
